{"answerText":"**No—on the stated tables-and-chairs arrangement, it does not need to be classified as Group A-3 if it is an assembly space accessory to the apartment-building occupancy.** Table 1004.1.3 assigns an assembly-without-fixed-seats, unconcentrated (tables and chairs) factor of 15 net square feet per occupant. At 900 net square feet, the calculated occupant load is 900 ÷ 15 = **60 occupants**, fewer than 75. An accessory assembly room with an occupant load below 75 must be classified as Group B or as part of the occupancy it serves, although its plumbing fixtures may be calculated under the assembly requirements (BC §§ 1004.1.3, 303.1.3).

Group A-3 otherwise includes recreation, amusement, and other assembly uses not classified elsewhere in Group A (BC § 303.4). The fact that use is limited to residents supports the stated residential-amenity context but does not by itself establish the required legal accessory relationship. If the room is not accessory, BC § 303.1.2 separately classifies a **building or nonaccessory tenant space** used for assembly purposes with fewer than 75 occupants as Group B; that provision does not itself expressly address a standalone nonaccessory room.

The classification must account for every intended use, not merely its normal furniture layout. If the room will also be used in an arrangement or for an activity producing an occupant load of 75 or more, or for a different purpose, the classification must be reevaluated for that use (BC § 302.1).","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"A-3 is the general assembly category","explanation":"Group A-3 includes assembly uses intended for worship, recreation, or amusement, along with other assembly uses not classified elsewhere in Group A.","sectionID":"135","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:135-1"]},{"heading":"Tables-and-chairs occupant-load calculation","explanation":"For assembly without fixed seats, the unconcentrated tables-and-chairs factor is 15 net square feet per occupant. Applied to the stated 900 net square feet, this produces an occupant load of 60 persons.","sectionID":"2152","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2152-2"]},{"heading":"Accessory assembly space below 75 occupants","explanation":"A room or space used for assembly purposes, with fewer than 75 occupants, and accessory to another occupancy must be classified as Group B or as part of that occupancy. Plumbing fixtures for that room or space may be calculated as assembly fixtures.","sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4"]},{"heading":"Nonaccessory building or tenant-space rule","explanation":"A building or nonaccessory tenant space used for assembly purposes with fewer than 75 occupants must be classified as Group B, with a permitted assembly-based plumbing-fixture calculation.","sectionID":"131","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:131-5"]},{"heading":"All intended purposes govern","explanation":"Structures and portions of structures must be classified by occupancy. A room or space intended for different purposes at different times must comply with the requirements applicable to each intended purpose.","sectionID":"128","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:128-3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["Whether the multipurpose room is legally accessory to the apartment-building occupancy. Resident-only use does not by itself establish that relationship.","Whether any intended alternate use, furniture layout, or event configuration changes the room’s occupant load or use from the stated unconcentrated tables-and-chairs arrangement."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied 2022 NYC enacted passages establish the below-75 accessory-room rule and the below-75 building/nonaccessory-tenant-space rule, but do not provide the full classification framework for a standalone room that is neither established as accessory nor a nonaccessory tenant space."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"2152","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:2152-2"],"relevance":"Provides the 15-net-square-foot occupant-load factor for unconcentrated assembly seating with tables and chairs."},{"sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-4"],"relevance":"Establishes the Group B or served-occupancy classification for an accessory assembly room below 75 occupants."},{"sectionID":"135","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:135-1"],"relevance":"Establishes the general scope of Group A-3 assembly occupancy."},{"sectionID":"131","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:131-5"],"relevance":"Establishes the below-75 Group B rule for a building or nonaccessory tenant space used for assembly purposes."},{"sectionID":"128","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:128-3"],"relevance":"Requires classification by occupancy and compliance with the requirements for each intended purpose of a multipurpose room."}]}

{"answerText":"**Not automatically.** If this is an **accessory assembly room** with fewer than 75 occupants, its Group B classification does not establish that the normal Group B fixture row may be used. Instead, BC § 303.1.3 expressly permits the required fixture count for that room or space to be calculated under the **Assembly** requirements. The supplied evidence does not establish that a normal Group B calculation is also permitted for that accessory-room path. (BC § 303.1.3; PC § 403.1)

PC Table 403.1 generally ties fixture minimums to the Building Code occupancy classification and occupant load. Its Group B footnote separately permits an **Assembly** fixture calculation for a building or nonaccessory tenant space used for assembly by fewer than 75 persons and classified as Group B under BC § 303.1.2; that separate, expressly limited permission is not authority for an accessory room. (PC § 403.1)

For an Assembly calculation, divide the total occupant load equally between sexes unless approved statistical data supports another distribution; apply each fixture-type ratio to each sex; and round fractions up. For multiple occupancies, sum the fractional results by occupancy before rounding up. (PC § 403.1.1) Fixtures in qualifying family/assisted-use rooms may count toward either sex for assembly occupancies, and fixtures in single-occupant toilet rooms may count toward either sex. (PC §§ 403.1.2, 403.1.3)","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Accessory assembly rooms retain an Assembly fixture-calculation option","explanation":"A room or space used for assembly, with fewer than 75 occupants, that is accessory to another occupancy is classified as Group B or as part of that occupancy; nevertheless, its plumbing fixtures are permitted to be calculated using the Assembly requirements. This classification rule does not itself establish permission to use the ordinary Group B fixture row instead.","sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-2"]},{"heading":"Fixture table relies on Building Code classification and occupant load","explanation":"Plumbing fixtures must be provided by occupancy type and in the Table 403.1 minimum numbers. Occupant load and occupancy classification are determined under the New York City Building Code. The table separately permits an Assembly calculation for a building or nonaccessory tenant space used for assembly by fewer than 75 persons and classified as Group B under BC Section 303.1, Exception 2; that stated subject does not establish the same permission for an accessory room.","sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-1"]},{"heading":"Calculation method and qualifying fixture credits","explanation":"The calculation ordinarily divides total occupant load equally between sexes, applies each fixture ratio to each sex, and rounds fractions up; for multiple occupancies, fractional results are summed before rounding. Approved statistical data can support a different sex distribution. Fixtures in qualifying family or assisted-use rooms may count for either sex in assembly occupancies, and fixtures in single-occupant toilet rooms may count for either sex.","sectionID":"11910","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11910-5"]},{"heading":"Family or assisted-use room fixture credit","explanation":"Fixtures located within family or assisted-use toilet and bathing rooms required by BC Section 1109.2.1 may be included in the required fixture count for either male or female occupants in assembly and mercantile occupancies.","sectionID":"11911","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11911-4"]},{"heading":"Single-occupant toilet room fixture credit","explanation":"Fixtures in single-occupant toilet rooms may be included in the fixture count required by PC Section 403 for either male or female occupants. Fixtures in toilet rooms subject to the PC Section 403.2.1 exception may count only for the applicable sex.","sectionID":"11912","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11912-3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":[],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied enacted evidence establishes the accessory-room Assembly-calculation option but does not expressly state whether ordinary Group B ratios remain independently available for that same accessory-room path. It also does not provide the room's final occupant load, proposed fixture arrangement, or any approved statistical sex-distribution data needed for a numerical fixture count."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"132","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:132-2"],"relevance":"Direct governing rule for accessory assembly spaces below 75 occupants and their Assembly fixture-calculation option."},{"sectionID":"11909","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11909-1"],"relevance":"Governing fixture table and its limited nonaccessory Group B/Assembly calculation footnote."},{"sectionID":"11910","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11910-5"],"relevance":"Governing calculation and rounding method."},{"sectionID":"11911","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11911-4"],"relevance":"Governing conditional credit for qualifying family or assisted-use rooms."},{"sectionID":"11912","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:11912-3"],"relevance":"Governing conditional credit for fixtures in single-occupant toilet rooms."}]}
